# Backend Architecture

## Structure

```
backend/src/
├── config/        DB, environment and app configuration
├── constants/     application-wide constants
├── models/        Mongoose schemas only — fields, validation, indexes, DB-specific behavior
├── controllers/   thin request/response handling
├── routes/        HTTP method + endpoint + middleware + controller mapping only
├── middleware/    authenticate, authorize, validate, error handler, request processing
├── services/      business logic (membership, attendance, email, report calculations)
├── utils/         generic helpers
├── validations/   request validation schemas
├── templates/     reusable email templates
├── helpers/
├── app.js
└── server.js
```

## Request flow — always

Route → Middleware → Controller → Service → Model → Database

```js
// routes: definition only, no logic
router.get("/members", authenticate, authorize(USER_ROLES.ADMIN), memberController.getMembers);
```

- **Controllers**: receive request → obtain/validate data → call service → return response → handle expected errors. No large workflows.
- **Services**: all business logic. Attendance calculations, membership expiry logic, report filtering and
  email sending live here and are never duplicated across controllers.
- **Models**: schema + validation + indexes. No application logic.
- **Routes**: never contain business logic.

## API response format — identical across every endpoint

```json
{ "success": true,  "message": "Member fetched successfully", "data": {} }
{ "success": false, "message": "Unable to fetch member",      "error": {} }
```

## Error handling

Centralized error middleware. Correct status codes: 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden,
404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Validation Error, 500 Internal Server Error.
Log useful server-side errors via the logging utility; never leak internals to clients.

## Authentication & authorization (enforced on the backend, never only on the frontend)

- JWT auth with roles `USER_ROLES.ADMIN` / `USER_ROLES.MEMBER`. bcrypt password hashing — never plain text.
- Implement login, logout, protected routes, role-based authorization, forgot/reset/change password, token validation.
- Admin-only endpoints: members, plans, attendance management, announcements, gym settings, reports, admin management.
- Members may only read their own profile, membership and attendance. Never allow access to another member's data.

## Security checklist for every endpoint

Authentication · authorization · request validation (validations/ + middleware) · input sanitization ·
CORS configuration · rate limiting where appropriate · secure HTTP headers · no error leakage ·
never expose JWT secret, SMTP password, MongoDB credentials or API keys.

## Database

Collections: `Admin`, `Member`, `MembershipPlan`, `MembershipPayment`, `Attendance`, `Announcement`, `GymSettings`.
Use references between collections. Index frequently searched fields (email, member ID, attendance date,
membership status). Paginate large datasets. Avoid unnecessary queries. Schema validation is mandatory;
request validation and frontend validation are additional layers, not replacements.

## Email

One reusable `services/email.service.js` with a single Nodemailer transport configured from env vars.
Templates live in `templates/`; never embed large HTML strings or create Nodemailer configs in controllers.
Supported emails: new member registration, membership expiry reminder on the end date, announcement,
and password reset. Email subjects are constants.
